  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find  the global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 2.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all real values of $x.$ So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ \n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{1}{2})=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14150956/assets/aef1d6e33213def71ada4a59f7d79b9f045083b2c501a752e81994e33f100277.png)",
